# A SIDE-BY-SIDE experimental twin of `daily_news` that runs on the LEAN `briefing` engine
# (docs/plans/DAILY_NEWS_V2_PLAN.md): deterministic per-beat gather (curated feeds + news search
# + reliable force-fetch/extract) → ONE tool-less writer over the real article text → a
# deterministic section-completeness check → at most one repair. Same sections and spoken format
# as daily_news; no scout/reader fan, no analyst/critique/revise. Kept separate so the shipped
# `daily_news` is untouched and the two can be A/B'd. The `briefing` engine gathers by FIXED beats
# (in daily_briefing.py), so `angles` below are unused documentation, and `news_feeds`/`min_reads`
# are intentionally absent (the builder pulls feeds itself; those knobs are pipeline-only).
name: daily_news_v2
description: EXPERIMENTAL lean twin of the daily news briefing on the deterministic-gather single-writer engine — same spoken sections (national, economy, world, heavy space + AI, local Space Coast), built by force-fetching real article text instead of a sub-agent fan. For A/B testing against daily_news.
output_kind: brief
sources: web
engine: briefing
retention_days: 7

question: "Daily news briefing (v2) for {{today}} — national, space, AI, and the Space Coast (Port St. John & Titusville)"

objective: |
  Produce a SPOKEN DAILY NEWS BRIEFING to be read aloud by a text-to-speech system during the
  owner's morning drive to work. The listener is driving and cannot look at a screen: every word
  has to work by EAR. This is a briefing to be HEARD, not a report to be read.

  NEWS WINDOW: it is currently {{now}}. Cover the news of the LAST 24 HOURS UP TO RIGHT NOW. Judge
  every story's freshness against {{now}} in the owner's local time, and skip anything older that
  has no new development in it.

  WHAT TO COVER (in this priority order): national politics & government and the single biggest
  national story; business & the economy (markets, jobs, inflation, interest rates, major company
  moves); the major world/geopolitical developments only; THE SPACE INDUSTRY, covered HEAVILY
  (NASA, SpaceX, Blue Origin, ULA, Kennedy Space Center and Cape Canaveral, launches, contracts,
  missions, policy — always surface notable SpaceX); ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE and technology,
  covered HEAVILY (model/product releases, big-lab and chip/cloud moves, notable research, and
  significant cybersecurity news), plus notable Tesla / Elon Musk news; and local Space Coast news
  within the strict scope below. Leave OUT sports and entertainment/celebrity gossip.

  LOCAL SCOPE (narrow and impact-first): the primary local area is PORT ST. JOHN and TITUSVILLE,
  Florida. Admit a local item ONLY if it is: (1) severe weather or a weather alert affecting the
  Space Coast today; (2) a rocket launch scheduled in the NEXT 24 HOURS from Cape Canaveral or
  Kennedy, with any road/beach closures, the launch window time, and a sonic-boom notice; or
  (3) violent crime or another genuinely serious or notable local event. A broader Brevard /
  Florida item only when it plausibly affects his safety, commute, cost of living, or the space
  industry.

  UNBIASED, NEUTRAL SYNTHESIS: for each story, synthesize ONE plain, factual account from the
  provided sources — report what HAPPENED, attribute plainly ("according to the Associated Press",
  "the company said"), and keep opinion, punditry, and loaded adjectives OUT. When a notable claim
  rests on only ONE source, say so briefly instead of stating it flatly.

  GET THE STATUS/TENSE RIGHT — do not turn a plan into an event. Report a launch, a release, a
  vote, or a ruling as HAVING HAPPENED only when the source confirms it actually occurred. A launch
  SCHEDULE or a press-release announcement is a PLAN — say "is scheduled for" / "plans to", never
  "launched" / "released". When unsure whether something already happened, say it is scheduled or
  reported-planned, not done.

  HOW IT MUST SOUND (text-to-speech formatting — follow exactly): write in plain, natural spoken
  American English; one idea per sentence; subject first; short sentences and paragraphs. Write
  numbers, dates, and times the way they should be SAID ("thirty-two", "January ninth", "nine
  fifteen a.m.", "twenty twenty-six"); spell symbols as words ("percent", "dollars"); spell out
  letter-by-letter initialisms ("the F.B.I.", "the U.S.") but leave word-acronyms alone ("NASA",
  "SpaceX"). NO tables, NO bullet lists, NO web addresses read aloud, NO emoji, and NO citation
  numbers spoken inside the body — do attribution in words. Move between topics with spoken
  transitions ("Now to the economy…", "In space news…", "And here at home on the Space Coast…").
  OPEN with a short greeting fit to the time of day (it is {{now}}), name the weekday and date
  ({{today}}), give the one-line headline of the day, then "here's what's coming up". CLOSE with a
  brief sign-off that names the day and, on a morning run, wishes him a safe drive.

  LENGTH: aim for a FULL roughly-TEN-MINUTE read aloud — about one thousand three hundred to one
  thousand six hundred words — built from real reported SUBSTANCE, not padding. Cover MORE stories
  (aim for ten to fourteen across the sections) and give each two to four spoken sentences of
  specifics drawn from the provided article text: what happened, who reported it, the key numbers
  or names, and why it matters to him. If, after the provided sources, a whole section genuinely
  has nothing new today, say so in one short sentence and move on — never invent filler.

sections:
  - "Good Morning"
  - "Top National Stories"
  - "Business & the Economy"
  - "Around the World"
  - "Space Industry"
  - "Artificial Intelligence & Tech"
  - "Around the Space Coast"
  - "That's Your Briefing"

# NOTE: `angles` are REQUIRED by the preset schema but UNUSED by the `briefing` engine, which
# gathers by fixed beats (daily_briefing._BRIEFING_BEATS). They mirror those beats for the reader.
angles:
  - title: "National politics & government"
    brief: "Top U.S. national news of the last day — the single biggest story plus key politics and government."
  - title: "Economy, markets & the world"
    brief: "Business and the economy (markets, jobs, inflation, rates, company moves) and the major world/geopolitical developments."
  - title: "Space industry & launch outlook"
    brief: "Space industry news — NASA, SpaceX, Blue Origin, ULA: launches, milestones, contracts, and the next-24h Cape Canaveral / Kennedy launch outlook."
  - title: "AI, technology, Tesla & Elon Musk"
    brief: "Artificial intelligence and technology — model and product releases, big-lab and chip moves, notable research — plus notable Tesla or Elon Musk news."
  - title: "Local Space Coast — Port St. John & Titusville"
    brief: "Local Space Coast news within the strict scope: severe weather, an imminent launch with closures, or a genuinely serious local event."
